      "dom_id": "question-94699",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Find the center of the elliptic cone $8(x-6)^{2}+12(y-4)^{2}=4(z+2)^{2}.$",
      "worked_solution": "The general equation of an elliptic cone with center at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-{x}_{0})^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-{y}_{0})^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=\\frac{(z-{z}_{0})^{2}}{{c}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo find the center, we rewrite our equation in standard form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8(x-6)^{2}+12(y-4)^{2} & =4(z+2)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{1/8}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{1/12} & =\\frac{(z+2)^{2}}{1/4} \\\\ \\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{1/8}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{1/12} & =\\frac{(z-(-2))^{2}}{1/4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic cone that has its center at $(6,4,-2).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [],
      "answer_fields_complete": null,
